plan Operations
title   Operations: reads, commits, streams and activity lanes
status  proposed
goal
    App authors declare keyed reads, explicit commits and continuing streams. Readers share
    current data, edits survive racing reads, and every admitted wait has an honest ending.
outcomes
    O1  Descriptors and keys
        resource({ id, key?, lookup, result?, staleTime, gcTime, persist? }),
        transaction({ id, key?, input?, commit, concurrency }) and
        stream({ id, key?, subscribe, result?, persist? }) return inert descriptors; constructing
        one starts no work. Descriptor ids follow @plan.Machines.O1; distinct descriptors with
        the same id reachable from one App fail construction. Runtime activation is 
        @plan.Runtime.O1.

        lookup(params, { signal }) and commit(params, { signal, attempt, basedOn }) return Effects;
        subscribe(params, { signal }) returns a Stream. Parameter, value, error and service types
        remain intact through declarations and use; service provision follows @plan.Runtime.O2.
        result and input are Effect Schemas. Persisted resources and streams require result;
        domain reads require it under @plan.EntityDomains.O1. Boundary transfer without result
        follows @plan.ServerRendering.O3. Typed errors never cross those boundaries.
        persist defaults to false; transactions have no persist option. Storage is 
        @plan.Persistence.O2.

        staleTime and gcTime are required nonnegative Duration inputs; invalid values fail
        construction. Zero means immediately stale or collected at the next turn boundary after
        the last hold ends. Infinity disables time-based staleness or collection, respectively.
        concurrency is required and is reject, cancel, allow or serialize.

        key(params) is a pure synchronous record projector; omission means {}. Equal records
        identify the same resource entry or transaction lane within one Runtime, regardless of
        member order; streams use @O4. Keys accept null, booleans, finite numbers, strings, dense
        arrays and plain records. Undefined record properties are absent; undefined array items,
        cycles, accessors, non-plain objects, lone surrogates and unsupported values are rejected
        without running getters or coercion. Negative zero equals zero; Unicode spelling matters.
        Keys allow at most 16 nested levels, 256 value nodes and 8,192 UTF-8 bytes of canonical 
        text.
        Invalid keys report the offending path and change nothing before work starts. In actions
        they reject the batch; in activities they report an issue and leave the lane undefined;
        passive reads throw. Later mutation of parameters cannot change an admitted key.

        invalidate and clear match a descriptor plus a partial key: every named property must
        equal the entry's whole property value; {} matches the family. Authors must include every
        parameter affecting the result, including identity and permission scope. Keys are exposed
        in persistence, payloads and traces; they neither authorize work nor reconstruct parameters.
    O2  Resources
        Equal descriptor and key share data and one in-flight lookup across actors in a Runtime.
        An entry is fresh only while it has confirmed data, is not invalidated and its age is less
        than staleTime. Separate Runtimes share nothing.

        O.x.lookup(select, options) returns fresh data without a request, joins an in-flight
        lookup, or starts one. O.x.refetch(select, options) always starts a replacement request;
        existing waiters join the replacement and the old request cannot install its result.
        O.x.subscribe(select, options) binds an activity and starts a request only when missing
        or stale with none running. Time passing alone starts nothing. Automatic requests use
        the oldest live binding's parameters; explicit requests use the caller's. A running
        request retains its starting parameters even if that binding leaves.

        Focus regained and connection restored refetch stale or missing subscribed entries with
        no request running, ordered by descriptor id then key. Offline status blocks and cancels
        nothing. Invalidation and clear follow @O5; domain repair follows @plan.EntityDomains.O1.
        Flow never automatically retries a failed lookup.

        Each finite lookup or refetch settles once as success(A), failure, defect or interrupt;
        success carries current data even when ordering rejects its request's result. Equal data
        still settles finite work. State exit does not cancel finite work; its event reaches the
        actor's later state. Actor disposal ends delivery. Continuing outcomes are value(A),
        failure, defect and interrupt. Each outcomes member is optional; tagged failure mappings
        must cover every error tag, untagged mappings receive E, and E = never forbids failure.
        Mappers receive values only. Unmapped failure or defect leaves its lane tag and an issue.

        value fires for confirmed data at binding and subsequently only when it changes by
        Equal.equals; optimistic values and predictions never fire it. Restored or hydrated
        bindings do not remap an already mapped version (@plan.Persistence.O4, 
        @plan.ServerRendering.O4).
        A live subscription, request or optimistic layer holds an entry. After its last hold,
        gcTime on the Runtime Clock removes it; later use starts missing. Releasing one owner
        preserves shared work until the last owner leaves. Passive reads never hold or start work.
        Cleared, collected or replaced requests cannot later change data or replacement bindings.
    O3  Transactions and commits
        Only an accepted action admits O.x.commit(select, { optimistic?, writes?, outcomes? });
        activation, restore, hydration and state re-entry never replay a commit. select reads the
        pre-turn snapshot and event. optimistic applies on start; writes receives { value } and
        applies only on success, before its outcome event, even after the issuing state exits.
        Outcomes are success(A), failure, defect, interrupt and unknown(Attempt), with @O2 mappings.

        Every admitted commit has an exported Attempt value { id, operation, key, input }.
        Flow mints a unique id; adapters receive it for remote idempotency. Input uses the input
        Schema or a structural encoding; unencodable input rejects admission before work starts.
        Domain prediction versions arrive as basedOn under @plan.EntityDomains.O1; otherwise {}.
        Attempt durability before invocation and recovery follow @plan.Persistence.O6.

        Concurrency is per actor, descriptor and key; other actors and keys are independent.
        reject declines a second unsettled commit without work or outcome; the action continues.
        cancel ends the previous commit under @O7 before admitting the next. allow starts every
        commit independently and delivers every outcome in settlement order. serialize starts
        commits FIFO, applying optimistic writes only on start; at most 64 queued occurrences
        are allowed. Overflow rejects the whole action batch before any work starts.

        Every admitted commit settles once. Its optimistic changes and success writes publish
        together before its event, so the handler sees the updated cache. Invalid success writes
        apply nothing and report an issue; the commit still succeeds. During actor disposal,
        cache consequences still apply but outcome events are dropped.
        Once the adapter has been called, cancellation or abandonment reports unknown with its
        Attempt: the remote write may have happened. Flow promises no remote rollback and never
        retries automatically. Queued, never-invoked work cannot become a remote write on restore;
        its restored ending is interrupt. Recorded unfinished attempts restore as unknown unless
        a durable settled result is known; neither restore nor hydration invokes a commit.

        O.x.retry(select, options) accepts an Attempt and reuses its exact id and decoded input
        under the same concurrency, writes and outcome rules. A known settled result settles
        immediately with its consequences and no adapter call. A foreign descriptor or invalid
        input rejects the action batch. Late completion of replaced work cannot deliver another
        outcome or affect a later actor incarnation.
    O4  Streams
        Each activity owns its session. Unchanged declaration, descriptor and key keep the
        session and its original parameters; changing identity releases it before opening another.
        Equal keys in different actors have independent sessions. A second same-actor session
        for the same descriptor and key reports an issue, leaves its lane undefined and preserves
        the existing session.

        Each emission replaces the latest lane value; it does not write resource data. Domain
        listeners follow @plan.EntityDomains.O1. With value mapped, the next value is pulled only
        after the previous event's turn, with at most one value event waiting per session. Equal
        values are silent. Without value mapped, lane updates may coalesce and run no transitions;
        slow readers may skip intermediate values. There is no history or replay.
        complete, failure, defect and adapter interrupt end the session once, retain its data
        and map their optional outcomes. A terminal session restarts only on identity change or
        state re-entry. Planned release maps no outcome; released sessions cannot publish later.
        Persisted latest values and session reopening follow @plan.Persistence.O4.
    O5  Writes and optimistic values
        O.x.setData(select) returns { key, data }; data is a value or updater(current) => A | 
        undefined.
        Updaters read confirmed data; undefined declines the write. O.x.invalidate(select) and
        O.x.clear(select) return partial keys. A source replaces data only if its request started
        at or after the stored data landed. setData starts and lands when applied, so older
        lookups cannot overwrite it. Accepted equal writes refresh freshness without value events.

        A write batch validates all targets before mutation and publishes atomically. More than
        256 expanded identities or any invalid target rejects the entire batch; no match is a
        successful no-op. invalidate retains data and optimistic values, marks entries stale and
        refetches subscribed entries with no request running. A request started before invalidation
        cannot make the entry fresh. clear removes data and optimistic values, blocks old results
        and makes surviving subscriptions Pending before reacquiring. Related commits still settle.
        Domain write restrictions and predictions are @plan.EntityDomains.O1.

        Every reader sees confirmed data with live optimistic updates applied in start order.
        Base changes and removal of one update preserve the others; pending remains true while
        any update is live. Optimistic values never become confirmed data or cross persistence
        or hydration boundaries. Failure, defect, interrupt and pre-invocation cancellation remove 
        that
        commit's updates without overwriting newer data. Success retains them until setData or
        a lookup started after settlement confirms the entry. unknown retains them and invalidates
        the entry until a confirming lookup lands. clear removes them with the entry.
    O6  Activity lanes
        snapshot.activities.<name> reflects that snapshot's publication and keeps object identity
        until its entry or session changes. It is undefined for inactive activities, when false,
        or null, undefined or false parameters. Transactions have outcomes, never lanes.
        Resource tags are Pending (no confirmed data), Ready (confirmed data, no request),
        Refreshing (confirmed data with a request), Failure (typed error), Defect and Interrupted
        (adapter interruption). Failure, Defect and Interrupted retain previous data; Refreshing
        always has data. All carry data and pending, true for live optimistic values or predictions.
        Stream tags are Running, Complete, Failure, Defect and Interrupted; data is the latest
        value or undefined, with no pending field. Undefined emissions need an author wrapper
        if they must be distinguished from no value. Domain lane additions are 
        @plan.EntityDomains.O1.

        placeholder({ previous, input }) may supply Pending data when a new key has no data;
        placeholder: true marks it. previous is this actor/activity's last input and lane data,
        retained across exit and re-entry. Placeholders never enter the store or persistence.
        descriptor.get(snapshot, keyInput) passively returns the lane-shaped view at that
        snapshot's store revision, or undefined. A stream read sees only that snapshot actor's
        session. It creates, holds, refreshes and starts nothing; invalid keys throw.
    O7  Cancellation and cleanup
        Planned release aborts the signal and runs finalizers once without interrupt outcomes;
        adapter-reported interruption maps interrupt. Releasing a subscription stops shared
        work only at its last owner; disposed actors receive no finite lookup outcome.
        O.x.cancel(select) cancels this actor's queued and running commits for a key. Resources
        have no cancel action. Before invocation, cancellation is silent and removes optimistic
        updates; after invocation it reports unknown and blocks late results. Actor disposal
        waits for invoked commits; bounded disposal follows @plan.Runtime.O6.
        Cleanup failure reports an issue without undoing a published transition. Disposal
        failure follows @plan.Runtime.O6; Cause handling follows @plan.Diagnostics.O3. After
        cleanup, late results cannot change data, lanes, outcomes or replacement work.
verification
    V1  Type proofs preserve adapter types and reject invalid options and outcome mappings.
    V2  Key tests prove equality, matching, bounds and rejection before any side effect.
    V3  Stories prove shared reads, Runtime isolation and passive snapshot reads.
    V4  Clock Stories prove freshness, collection, refetch triggers and late-result rejection.
    V5  Stories prove atomic batch limits, invalidation repair and edits surviving older reads.
    V6  Stories prove commit policies, queue limits and cache writes before outcome handlers.
    V7  Stories prove optimistic stacking, independent removal and confirmation after success.
    V8  Stories prove unknown, no restore replay and retry preserving the Attempt and result.
    V9  Stories prove stream isolation, duplicate rejection, bounded delivery and safe release.
    V10  Stories prove lane tags, retained data, placeholder isolation and historical reads.
questions
    Q1  Deferred to build time
        Choose key encoding and validation mechanics.
        Choose internal request and settlement identities and capacity accounting.
        Choose Attempt id generation and structural input codec details.
        Choose store layout, batch traversal and publication mechanics.
        Choose host-signal wiring.
        Choose stream buffering integration and cleanup instrumentation.
        Choose lint warnings and documentation examples.
